<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bourgeoisie</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as bourgeois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The bourgeoisie is a social class of business owners, merchants and wealthy people that emerged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> as a middle class between the peasantry and the aristocracy. The word comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> borgeis, 'town dweller', from bourg, 'market town': the medieval bourgeois were the craftsmen, artisans and merchants of the towns, which grew into cities of commerce and crafts from the 11th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, the American War of Independence and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> were partly motivated by their wish to be rid of feudal and royal limits on property and trade. In Marxist theory it owns the means of production and exploits the labour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie.jpg" alt="Bourgeoisie" width="330" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Béraud · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Béraud_-_La_Sortie_du_Bourgeois.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For it were a petty <b>bourgeoisie</b> of lawyers such as Moreno, Castelli, Belgrano and Paso, popular priests, doctors and common workers like Domingo French and Antonio Beruti, and a new merchant bourgeoisie of criollo and British houses whose trade with other countries, long treated as smuggling, had been…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Communists hold that the present order stems from capitalism, which divides society into two classes: the proletariat, the majority, who must sell their labour power to survive, and the <b>bourgeoisie</b>, a minority that profits from owning the means of production.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>At the top of the Third Estate stood the <b>bourgeoisie</b>: merchants and bankers, financiers and tax farmers, lawyers, notaries and doctors, the holders of venal offices, and rentiers living on their investments.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
